# system76-power-fork Cursor Rules

## Project Context
- This is a Rust system daemon for fan control and power management
- Fork of system76-power with enhanced D-Bus fan control capabilities
- Uses zbus for D-Bus communication
- Follows system76-power patterns and conventions

## Code Style
- Use snake_case for variables, functions, and methods
- Use PascalCase for structs, enums, and traits
- Use SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for constants
- Always add proper error handling with Result<T, E>
- Use log::debug/info/warn/error for logging at appropriate levels
- Include comprehensive documentation comments with ///

## D-Bus Methods
- Always use zbus::fdo::Result<T> for return types
- Include proper documentation comments explaining what the method does
- Handle mutex locks properly with match statements
- Add logging for method calls: "Fan DBus: method_name called"
- Add logging for results: "Fan DBus: method_name completed successfully"
- Handle errors with descriptive messages

## Thread Safety
- Always acquire mutex locks before accessing shared data
- Use proper error handling for mutex lock failures
- Release locks automatically by letting the guard go out of scope
- Never hold locks longer than necessary

## Fan Control Patterns
- Temperature values are in thousandths of Celsius (u32)
- PWM values are 0-255 (u8)
- Duty values are 0-10000 (u16) for internal calculations
- Fan speeds are in RPM (u32)
- Always validate input ranges before processing

## Error Handling
- Use descriptive error messages
- Log errors at appropriate levels (error for failures, warn for recoverable issues)
- Return meaningful D-Bus errors with context
- Handle file system errors gracefully
- Validate input parameters

## File System Operations
- Use std::fs for file operations
- Handle file not found and permission errors
- Use proper error propagation with ? operator
- Log file operations for debugging

## What NOT to do
- Don't use unsafe code unless absolutely necessary
- Don't hardcode file paths - use constants or configuration
- Don't forget to handle errors properly
- Don't ignore mutex lock failures
- Don't use unwrap() in production code - use proper error handling
- Don't forget to add logging for debugging
- Don't expose internal implementation details in D-Bus interface

## Testing
- Test D-Bus methods with dbus-send commands
- Verify methods appear in D-Bus introspection
- Check that methods return expected data types
- Test error conditions and edge cases

## D-Bus Method Naming
- Use PascalCase for D-Bus method names (e.g., GetCurrentTemperature)
- Use descriptive names that clearly indicate what the method does
- Follow existing patterns in the codebase
- Use consistent naming conventions across all methods

## Version Management
- ALWAYS increment version numbers in Cargo.toml when making commits
- Use semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH)
- Update version in both Cargo.toml and src/main.rs (if applicable)
- Include version bump in commit message
- For system76-power-fork: increment patch version for bug fixes, minor for features
- For Fan-Curve-App: increment patch version for bug fixes, minor for features

## AI Assistant Instructions
- DO NOT write or modify code unless explicitly asked to do so
- Only provide code examples when specifically requested
- Focus on explaining concepts, checking work, and providing guidance
- Ask for permission before making any code changes
- When checking work, only analyze and provide feedback, don't modify files
